<div class="notification is-info is-light">
    <strong>Time axes need no extra script.</strong> From 2.0.0, <code>time</code> and <code>timeseries</code> axes work with only Chart.js and
    <code>blazorexpress.chartjs.js</code>: the library registers its own date adapter. Do not add a third-party date adapter such as
    <code>chartjs-adapter-date-fns</code>; the library's adapter replaces it. See the <a href="@DemoRouteConstants.Docs_LineChart#time-axis">time axis documentation</a>.
</div>

<section id="javascript-functions" class="content mb-5">
    <h3 class="be-release-title">JavaScript functions</h3>
    <p>
        Some Chart.js options take a JavaScript function: tick labels, tooltip text, legend filters and clicks, line segment styles, gradient colours and more.
        Chart.js calls these functions while it draws, so they must be JavaScript. From 2.0.0 you write them in your own JavaScript file, register each one by name,
        and reference it from C# with <code>ChartFunction</code>.
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>
            Create a JavaScript file in your app, for example <code>wwwroot/js/chart-functions.js</code>, and register your functions:
            <pre><code>blazorexpress.chartjs.registerFunction('formatCurrency', value =>
    new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', { style: 'currency', currency: 'USD' }).format(value));</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li>
            Load it after <code>blazorexpress.chartjs.js</code>, so <code>registerFunction</code> exists:
            in <code>wwwroot/index.html</code> for Blazor WebAssembly and .NET MAUI Blazor, or in <code>App.razor</code> (<code>Components/App.razor</code>) for Blazor Web Apps with Server or Auto render modes.
            <pre><code>&lt;script src="_content/BlazorExpress.ChartJS/blazorexpress.chartjs.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src="js/chart-functions.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li>
            Reference the function by its registered name:
            <pre><code>options.Scales.Y.Ticks = new ChartAxesTicks { Callback = new ChartFunction("formatCurrency") };</code></pre>
        </li>
    </ol>
    <ul>
        <li>For currency, percent and compact axis labels, <code>Ticks.Format</code> (<code>ChartNumberFormat</code>) needs no JavaScript at all.</li>
        <li>Value properties such as <code>BackgroundColor</code> have a function sibling, such as <code>BackgroundColorFunction</code>, which is sent instead when set.</li>
        <li>A name that isn't registered logs one console error naming the function and the option; the option keeps its Chart.js default. A function you register later is used at the next <code>UpdateAsync</code>.</li>
        <li>A legend <code>OnClick</code> function replaces Chart.js's default toggle, as in Chart.js. The library still keeps the visibility it changed and still raises <code>OnLegendClick</code>.</li>
        <li>If a registered function throws, the error is logged to the console once and the chart keeps drawing.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="notification is-warning is-light">
        <strong>Security:</strong> only functions you register can be referenced. A name is never evaluated as code, and globals such as <code>window.open</code> or <code>fetch</code> can't be reached by name.
        Register only functions meant to be called with chart data, never general-purpose functions such as navigation or network calls, because chart options can carry values that came from your users.
    </div>
</section>

